// endocannabinoid.mjs — the endocannabinoid-modulation shelf of the MELEK hemp-science section.
//
// WHAT THIS SHELF IS. A research reference on the endocannabinoid system treated as a system to be
// MODULATED rather than replaced: the two principal ligands and their separate synthesis and
// degradation routes, the two receptors, the two hydrolases, the contested membrane-transport step,
// and the botanical chemistry that acts on any of them. The intellectual centre of the shelf is one
// distinction, stated on the overview page and carried through every other page: raising the tone of
// an endogenous signalling system by slowing its degradation is pharmacologically a DIFFERENT
// proposition from adding an exogenous agonist, and the difference is a ceiling.
//
// WHERE THE MATERIAL COMES FROM. Primary literature, every identifier in CITES below resolved against
// the Crossref REST API on 2026-09-27 with the returned title recorded verbatim. Four DOIs in the
// first draft resolved to real but WRONG papers and were corrected. Three claims carried over from the
// operator's kava-potentiators datasheet and the Van Kush Family Temple Pharmacopoeia knowledgebase
// could not be traced to a primary source and are marked verified: false and labelled in the prose;
// one datasheet claim (8-prenylnaringenin as "the most potent natural MAGL inhibitor") is contradicted
// by the literature and is corrected on the natural-inhibitors page rather than repeated.
//
// WHAT THIS SHELF DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN. No preparative or synthetic procedures of any kind:
// no reagents, solvents, catalysts, temperatures, times, work-ups or yields; no extraction protocols;
// no precursor sourcing; no step-by-step for any conversion, isomerisation or acetylation. Synthesis
// and extraction literature is cited by reference only, never reproduced. No dosing protocols and no
// preparation instructions, including on the kava page, whose source datasheet contains both — the
// mechanisms and the hazards are here, the handling arithmetic belongs on the formulation shelf. No
// medical advice: this describes what is documented, not what anyone should do.

export const SHELF = Object.freeze({
  id: 'endocannabinoid',
  title: 'Endocannabinoid Modulation',
  blurb: 'The receptors, the two hydrolases, the contested transport step, and the botanical chemistry that acts on them — with the ceiling that separates enzyme inhibition from direct agonism.',
  updated: '2026-09-27',
});

